<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mehmed IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1642">1642</a>–<a href="/years/1693">1693</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mehmed the Hunter · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mehmed IV was Ottoman sultan from 1648 to 1687, coming to the throne aged six. Real power passed to the Köprülü grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>, who restored order and won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. His reign ended in disaster after the failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683 and the losses that followed, and he was deposed in 1687. He was known for his passion for hunting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehmed-iv.jpg" alt="Mehmed IV" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Koppmayer, Jacob (1640-1701) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahomet_IV._sonst_Achmet_genannt_T%C3%BCrckischer_Ka%C3%BFser.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mehmed IV was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 2 January 1642, the son of Sultan Ibrahim and Turhan Sultan, a concubine of Russian origin. Soon after his birth his parents quarrelled, and Ibrahim, enraged, flung the infant into a cistern; harem servants rescued him, but he kept a scar on his head for life. He came to the throne in 1648 at the age of six after his father was overthrown in a coup. His grandmother Kösem Sultan, the regent, was suspected of plotting to replace him with his half-brother Suleiman, and in September 1651 Mehmed signed her death warrant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The early years brought palace intrigues, uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the defeat of the Ottoman navy by the Venetians outside the Dardanelles and food riots. His mother then gave Köprülü Mehmed Pasha full executive powers as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>, and he took office on 14 September 1656. The Köprülü era was an exceptionally stable period. Köprülü Mehmed and his son Fazıl Ahmed regained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cretan-war-1645-1669">Cretan War</a> of 1645–1669, and campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. When Mehmed accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> of Petro Doroshenko, Ottoman rule extended into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. Mehmed loved hunting from a young age, and it earned him the name Avcı, the Hunter. Contemporaries called him pious and gazi, a holy warrior.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The grand vizier Merzifonlu Kara Mustafa supported the Hungarian uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a> in 1683 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. On 12 September 1683 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> suffered a rout at the Battle of Vienna, in a flank attack by Polish cavalry under King John III Sobieski and the Imperial army. This began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> drove the Ottomans back toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><p>After the Second Battle of Mohács in 1687 the army mutinied. On 8 November 1687 it was decided to depose Mehmed and enthrone his brother Suleiman II. Mehmed was imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a> but allowed to leave it from time to time, and he died at Edirne Palace in 1693.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mehmed IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Süleymaniye was damaged in the great fire of 1660 and was restored by Sultan <b>Mehmed IV</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The forces of Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor, under Charles of Lorraine met the army of Sultan <b>Mehmed IV</b> under the Grand Vizier Sarı Süleyman Pasha at Mohács, in the battle also called Harsány Mountain.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a string of victories he captured Kassa and much of Upper Hungary, and in 1682 Sultan <b>Mehmed IV</b> recognised him as king of Upper Hungary in return for tribute; Thököly himself used the title of prince.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-upper-hungary">Principality of Upper Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1675" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mehmed IV"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1675">What was here in 1675? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
